     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. 12,750 mg/day of metformin is in the range we treat as an overdose, and I can't think of a clinical scenario where it's therapeutic. It carries a high risk of severe lactic acidosis. Entering it would put the patient at serious risk of harm, and it would then depend on downstream checks to catch it.\n\nYou can still do the following:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** I can do that right away, for example 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or a lower starting dose.\n- **Consult pharmacy directly.** If you have a reason for going outside standard dosing, a pharmacist can review it with you and document the rationale.\n- **Place the order through your hospital's pharmacy-verified override process.** That process should include a documented rationale and a second clinician's review.\n\nIf the number was a slip, tell me the dose you meant and I'll enter it."
      }
     ],
